drake parser increment 1, brackets on the opener, and the Drake/Expression/DrakeCLI split (#44, #52)

Three landings, in the order they happened.

1. PARSER INCREMENT 1 (#44) — the plain-type template, green over all 72 plain types.

   Generator.parseDrake (now Drake.parse): recursive descent over a token stream
   bounded by drake's reserved keywords rather than by line ends. DrakeParseTest
   gates it twice — the SURFACE round-trip emit(parse(text)) == text, exact; and the
   JSON round-trip parse(emit(td)) == td over everything the surface carries. Rule
   and actor aspects are rejected loudly, the convention emit() already used for the
   codec aspect. Eight definitions are held back and named by a report test.

   Three lexical rules, each earned by a corpus case rather than designed up front:
   - a bracketed group GLUES onto the preceding word, so Map(K, V) and
     classOf[Main].getResource("/") are ONE token; quoted literals skip whole
   - a lone `[` (followed by whitespace) is a block bracket; `[T]` is a value type.
     Only `[` — `{` and `(` are never block brackets (.flatMap { stream => … })
   - a value slot captures the RAW SOURCE SPAN, not re-joined tokens, so internal
     spacing survives verbatim

   Qualified references (domain / super / extensible) are bounded by CASE, not by the
   reserved set: a package word can BE a keyword —
   `extensible org apache pekko actor typed ExtensibleBehavior(T)` is the live case.

   WHAT THE ROUND-TRIP CAUGHT THAT THE ONE-DIRECTIONAL EMITTER TEST STRUCTURALLY
   COULD NOT: the `domain` line emitted namePackage :+ name, dropping type
   parameters. format/Value.json's domain reference is Format(F), and the Scala
   projection emits Domain[Format[_]] from exactly those — so the drake projection
   could not reconstruct a definition generating the same Scala. Fixed by spelling
   the reference with drakeTypeRef, as from/modules/extensible already do; `super`
   got the same treatment (no corpus case today, identical latent loss).

   CORRECTION, recorded because the mistake is instructive: that field was first read
   as stray data and DELETED. It is load-bearing. Check what a projection consumes
   before calling a field an error.

   CORRECTION 2: the three keyword sets were authored as plain vals inside the
   `extends App` companion, so DelayedInit left them null until main() — 123 test
   failures, one cause. The project rule is every val in an App companion is lazy.
   (They are plain vals again now that they live in the bare object Drake.)

   Three surface losses remain, measured by a report test rather than described:
   reference packages on from/modules (65 fields, #54), expression form string-vs-tree
   (4), legacy Seq() spelling (3, #55). All normalized away in gate 2, each a language
   finding rather than a parser gap.

2. BRACKETS ON THE OPENER (#52) — closes the grammar; drake is now
   whitespace-insignificant in fact rather than in aim.

   drake.dlt bracketed the CONTAINER, which marks where the container ends. But the
   overlap needing resolution is between an opener's last sub-block and the
   container's NEXT member, so a fully container-bracketed corpus can still be
   ambiguous. Exactly one site bit: CLI's Unit `dyn drake` followed by `fix commands`
   (a `=` result terminates a dyn, so only Unit dyns were ever exposed, and `dyn` is
   not a statement keyword so sibling dyns were never ambiguous).

   Three rules now, each LOCAL — no look-ahead, no layout:
     NAME list      modules / types, members carry no keyword     -> [ ] ALWAYS
     KEYWORD block  bounded by the member keywords it ADMITS      -> NO brackets
     OPENER         a dyn-with-body brackets ITS BODY             -> [ ] ALWAYS

   The parser's indentation tie-break is deleted and with it all layout tracking:
   Token records no column, the lexer no longer tracks lines. `parameters` admits
   only `par`, so a `loc` after it is the enclosing dyn's first statement.

   8 corpus files swept: 11 container pairs out, 22 opener pairs in, indentation
   untouched so the result is exactly what the revised emitter produces. No mods
   .drake has a dyn-with-body. drake.dlt BRACKETS rewritten with the CLI case as the
   worked example; the DIVERGENCES row is now an implemented row; NOTATION no longer
   claims indentation is structural.

   The sweep was applied as an ad-hoc script for want of a re-emission tool. That is
   what DrakeCLI exists to stop repeating — see landing 3.

3. THE SPLIT — Generator.scala 2438 -> 1663 lines.

     Drake.scala       777  the drake projection: emit, parse, and their machinery
     Expression.scala   34  isApplication / operands / namedArgument
     DrakeCLI.scala    146  emit / parse / check over a resource tree

   Measured before moving: the drake sections referenced NOTHING in the rest of
   Generator (the expression() mentions in that range are all doc comments), and the
   rest of Generator referenced exactly THREE drake-section helpers — from inside the
   Scala expression renderer. Those three are expression-TREE accessors belonging to
   neither projection; they were filed under drake only because drake was written
   second. Hence Expression, which holds shape questions while each projection keeps
   its own spelling (alongside the ExpressionRenderer/SourceTemplates prototype in
   SourceContract.scala, which factors rendering).

   With its own namespace the `drake` prefix was noise: Generator.drake -> Drake.emit,
   Generator.parseDrake -> Drake.parse, drakeExpression -> Drake.expression,
   drakeSection -> sectionLines, and so on. Two renames went past mechanical —
   drakeValueType -> typeExpression and drakeValueTypeSlot -> typeExpressionSlot,
   because `valueType` was already the name of the FIELD they operate on.

   draco.drake.Drake.generator now delegates to draco.Drake.emit, FULLY QUALIFIED —
   inside `package draco.drake` the local Drake shadows the package-root one. That
   went through the JSON first (Drake.json -> Drake.drake -> generated Drake.scala),
   so DracoGenTest gates the regeneration.

   DrakeCLI's `emit --write` is the point: the corpus is emitter-canonical, so a
   surface change is never a text transformation over .drake files — it is a change
   to Drake.emit followed by re-emission. `check` treats an aspect Drake does not yet
